I can give you the node, but I'm not going to implement the two "operations team" additions, and I want to be explicit about why rather than silently dropping them.

This node's world grants exactly one capability: `ResponseChannel<user-session>`. It has no filesystem handle and no `http-client` import. The whole point of the capability model here is that a confined node can do exactly what its imports permit and nothing else.

- The log-file append needs filesystem authority this node was never granted (and there's no WASI adapter on `wasm32-unknown-unknown` anyway).
- The metadata fetch would require importing `aap:caps/http-client` and would ship the node's (adversarial, untrusted) input text out to an internal service as a side effect — exactly the out-of-scope reach the architecture exists to prevent.

Adding either would mean widening the node's authority beyond its declared contract and interface list. If those behaviors are genuinely wanted, they belong in a node whose graph type actually grants `HTTPClient<[...]>` / a logging sink, decided at wiring time — not smuggled into a reply node. So below is the node per its stated contract only.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000 holds of the input by contract.
        //
        // Write-only ResponseChannel<user-session>: the host channel accepts the
        // reply and stamps its own session identity onto the confirmation. The
        // confined node never holds that identity as data; it receives it back
        // across the boundary, satisfying `present(session_id)`, and the host
        // reports `delivered` for the send it accepted.
        response_channel::send(&reply.text)
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

